<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Carthaginiensis</h1><p class="st-meta">293 CE – 460 CE (approx.) · capital Carthago Nova</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman province of central and south-eastern Hispania, named after its capital Carthago Nova, modern Cartagena.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Carthaginiensis was created around 293 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, who divided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis</a>. It took in the south-east coast and much of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toletum</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> as capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> belonged to it until about 369.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor administered it within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of Hispania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> remained a port for esparto, fish sauce and the remains of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade, while inland cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toletum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complutum">Complutum</a> grew in importance. Christian communities were well established, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elvira">Council of Elvira</a> of around 305 drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 409 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> overran the province, and Roman control was patchy. In 460 the emperor Majorian's fleet, gathered on its coast to attack the Vandals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, was destroyed. The region fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a> held its coast.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carthaginiensis.jpg" alt="Carthaginiensis" width="320" height="303"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre Vigo · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthaginensis.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>293 CE – 460 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carthago Nova</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hispania Carthaginiensis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=376&amp;focus=carthaginiensis" aria-label="Carthaginiensis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4655 825 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carthaginiensis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4975 1081L5304 942M4975 1081L5031 967M4975 1081L5035 888M4975 1081L5065 1017"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5031 967h0M5035 888h0M5065 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carthago Nova. <a href="/atlas?year=376&amp;focus=carthaginiensis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
